IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (Western Australia) -- PART VIII, section 65: distance.
-- Sections 61 to 64 of Part VIII (time) are in wa-interpretation-time.l4.
-- For a person who must be a given distance from something (a boundary, a premises, a school) the section decides
-- which distance is measured: the straight line on a horizontal plane, not the road, and not the slope.

§ `Part VIII -- Provisions regarding time and distance (distance)`

§§ `65. Distance, measurement of`

-- A place is described by how far east and north it is from a datum, and its height above it.  Height is kept so that the
-- rule can be shown to ignore it.
DECLARE `A place on the ground` HAS
    `metres east of the datum`  IS A NUMBER
    `metres north of the datum` IS A NUMBER
    `metres above the datum`    IS A NUMBER

-- Section 65: the distance shall be measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane.  Both places are projected on to
-- the plane, so the height of each place drops out.
-- src:654 | In the measurement of any distance for the purposes of a written law, the distance shall be measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane.
@ref s 65 (src:654)
GIVEN `the first place` IS A `A place on the ground`
      `the second place` IS A `A place on the ground`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the distance measured for the purposes of a written law between` `the first place` `and` `the second place` MEANS
    SQRT (eastwards TIMES eastwards PLUS northwards TIMES northwards)
    WHERE
        eastwards MEANS `the second place`'s `metres east of the datum` MINUS `the first place`'s `metres east of the datum`
        northwards MEANS `the second place`'s `metres north of the datum` MINUS `the first place`'s `metres north of the datum`
